EXTRA_POSTS = [
 {'tag': 'Heating',
 'col': '#D7262B',
 'ic': 'furnace',
 'date': 'Nov 2026',
 'iso': '2026-11-26',
 'slug': 'replacing-a-furnace-in-saskatoon',
 't': 'Replacing a Furnace in Saskatoon: A Cold-Weather Checklist',
 'x': 'Planning a new furnace in Saskatoon or nearby towns? Sizing for deep cold, venting in heavy snow, the SaskEnergy rebate and what to ask before you sign.',
 'body': ['A furnace in Saskatoon works harder than almost anywhere in the country. When it fails in January, the house can drop to an unsafe temperature within hours. If yours is getting old, '
          'planning the replacement before it breaks gives you time to choose well instead of taking whatever is in stock that night.',
          '## Start with the right size',
          'Many older furnaces in Saskatoon homes are bigger than the house needs. An oversized furnace blasts heat, satisfies the thermostat in a few minutes and shuts off. Then the '
          'far bedrooms cool down and the cycle starts again. It burns more gas and wears out the igniter and blower sooner.',
          'Sizing should come from a room-by-room heat loss calculation that uses our local design temperature, your insulation, windows and air sealing. Homes that got new windows, attic insulation '
          'or spray foam in the basement often need a smaller furnace than the one they have. Ask how a quote was sized. A number copied from the old nameplate is a warning sign.',
          '## Venting that survives a prairie winter',
          'High-efficiency furnaces vent through two plastic pipes, one bringing in combustion air and one carrying exhaust out. In a Saskatoon winter those pipes face drifting snow and frost. When '
          'the intake or exhaust gets blocked, the furnace shuts itself off for safety, usually on the coldest night of the week.',
          'Good installs plan for this:',
          '- Terminations placed high enough above the expected snow line, away from where snow piles off the roof.',
          "- Kept clear of decks, gas meters, windows and the neighbour's side yard, following the gas code.",
          '- The right slope on the exhaust pipe so condensate drains back to the furnace instead of freezing at the wall.',
          '- A condensate drain line protected from freezing, especially in unheated crawl spaces or garages.',
          'After a big storm, walk around the house and brush snow away from both pipes. It takes a minute and prevents one of the most common causes of no-heat calls after a blizzard.',
          '## Two-stage, modulating or single-stage?',
          'A single-stage furnace is either fully on or off. A two-stage furnace runs on low fire most of the winter and switches to high only when the cold sets in. A modulating furnace adjusts in '
          'small steps. In a climate with long, steady cold, the two-stage and modulating options run longer, quieter cycles and keep temperatures more even between floors. The single-stage model '
          'costs less to buy and is simpler to repair.',
          '## The SaskEnergy furnace rebate',
          "[SaskEnergy's Residential Equipment Replacement Rebate](https://www.saskenergy.com/node/217) pays $325 for a natural gas furnace rated 97% AFUE or higher. The current terms took effect on "
          'August 17, 2026. The furnace has to be installed by a gas contractor with a valid TSASK licence, and the application has to reach SaskEnergy within 120 days of installation. Approved '
          'rebates are paid in about six weeks.',
          'We hold the licence, and your invoice includes the model and serial details the application asks for. Our [rebates page](rebates/) lists the other equipment SaskEnergy covers, such as '
          'tankless water heaters and boilers.',
          'If you are also thinking about cooling, a cold-climate heat pump paired with a gas furnace is worth pricing. The federal [National Heat Pump Rebate](national-heat-pump-rebate/) covers '
          'qualifying heat pumps installed from November 1, 2026.',
          '## Questions to ask before you sign',
          '1. How did you size the furnace, and can I see the calculation?',
          '2. Where will the intake and exhaust pipes exit, and how high above grade?',
          '3. Are the plenum and duct transition included, or extra?',
          '4. Who pulls the gas permit?',
          '5. Does the model qualify for the SaskEnergy rebate, and will the invoice show what the application needs?',
          '6. What is the parts and labour warranty, and do I need to register it?',
          '## Acreages and towns around Saskatoon',
          'Outside the city, many homes heat with propane, have longer vent runs or sit on open land where wind piles snow against the house. The same rules apply, with extra care for the vent '
          'location. We install furnaces in [Warman](service-areas/warman/), [Martensville](service-areas/martensville/), [Waldheim](service-areas/waldheim/), [Hepburn](service-areas/hepburn/) and '
          'other nearby towns, as well as across Saskatoon.',
          'See our [furnace page](furnaces/) for the models we install, or book a free in-home quote and we will measure, size and price the job in writing.'],
 'faq': [('How much is the SaskEnergy furnace rebate?',
          'SaskEnergy currently lists $325 for a natural gas furnace rated 97% AFUE or higher, installed by a TSASK-licensed contractor, with the application sent within 120 days.'),
         ('Why does my furnace shut off during snowstorms?',
          'Usually the intake or exhaust pipe outside is blocked by snow or frost. Clear both pipes gently. If it keeps happening, the pipes may need to be raised or moved.'),
         ('Do you install furnaces outside Saskatoon?', 'Yes. We work in Warman, Martensville, Osler, Dalmeny, Langham, Waldheim and other towns near Saskatoon, including acreages on propane.')]},
 {"tag": "Garage heat", "col": "#0B4F8A", "ic": "garage", "date": "Oct 2026", "slug": "garage-heater-sizing-saskatchewan",
  "t": "How big a garage heater do you need in Saskatchewan?", "x": "BTU sizing, gas vs. infrared vs. electric, and what to check before you buy.",
  "body": ["A heater that's fine in Vancouver will barely keep up in Saskatoon. Our design temperature is around −35 °C, so the heater has to replace the heat lost through the walls, ceiling and — most of all — the overhead door.",
           "As a starting point, an insulated double garage usually needs about 45,000 BTU of gas heat, an insulated triple garage 60,000–75,000 BTU, and an uninsulated or tall shop far more. We confirm it with a quick heat-loss calculation.",
           "Power-vented unit heaters like the Modine Hot Dawg are the most popular because they vent through the wall. Ceramic infrared heaters like the Re-Verber-Ray DR series recover fastest after the door opens because they warm the floor. Electric heaters are the simplest for small garages without gas.",
           "Every gas heater needs a properly sized gas line, venting, clearances and a TSASK gas permit — we handle all of it."]},
 {"tag": "Rebates", "col": "#2E7D32", "ic": "rebate", "date": "Oct 2026", "slug": "saskenergy-rebates-explained",
  "t": "SaskEnergy rebates explained", "x": "What the Residential Equipment Replacement Rebate pays and how to qualify.",
  "body": ["SaskEnergy's Residential Equipment Replacement Rebate pays homeowners back for installing high-efficiency natural gas equipment.",
           "Current amounts listed by SaskEnergy (terms effective August 17, 2026): $325 for a 97%+ AFUE furnace, $1,000 for a tankless water heater (0.87+ UEF), $1,000 for a condensing water heater (0.80+ UEF), $750 for a 95%+ boiler up to 300 MBH and $800 for a combi boiler.",
           "The unit must be installed by a contractor with a valid TSASK gas licence — self-installs don't qualify — and you must apply within 120 days of installation. Funding is limited and the program can change, so check before you start.",
           "When we quote, we tell you which rebate your equipment qualifies for and give you the invoice details the application asks for."]},
 {"tag": "Heating", "col": "#D7262B", "ic": "furnace", "date": "Oct 2026", "slug": "signs-you-need-a-new-furnace",
  "t": "7 signs it's time to replace your furnace", "x": "Age, noises, bills and repairs — when replacement beats another fix.",
  "body": ["Furnaces rarely fail without warning. These are the signs we see most often before a breakdown:",
           "1. Age. Many furnaces last around 15–20 years — fewer in Saskatchewan, where they work harder.",
           "2. Rising SaskEnergy bills without a change in how you use your home.",
           "3. Frequent repairs — if you've called for service two or three winters in a row, it's worth pricing a replacement.",
           "4. Uneven heat, especially a cold room over the garage.",
           "5. Short-cycling: the furnace turns on and off every few minutes.",
           "6. Noises like banging, squealing or rattling.",
           "7. A yellow or flickering burner flame, soot, or a carbon monoxide alarm — call a technician right away.",
           "We'll inspect your furnace and give you an honest repair-vs-replace answer with both prices in writing."]},
 {"tag": "Winter", "col": "#1E1E1E", "ic": "maint", "date": "Nov 2026", "slug": "frozen-furnace-vent-pipes",
  "t": "Frozen or blocked furnace vents at −35 °C", "x": "Why high-efficiency furnaces stop on the coldest nights — and how to prevent it.",
  "body": ["High-efficiency furnaces and water heaters vent through plastic pipes on the side of the house. On very cold nights frost can build up on the exhaust, and snow drifts can bury the intake.",
           "If your furnace stops in deep cold, check outside first: carefully brush snow away from both pipes and look for frost closing the openings. Never chip ice with a hammer — PVC cracks easily in the cold.",
           "Repeated freezing usually means the vent was installed too close to the ground, too long, or without the right slope. We can re-route it above the drift line or add a kit that reduces frosting.",
           "If clearing the vents doesn't restart the furnace, call us 24/7."]},
 {"tag": "Maintenance", "col": "#F96302", "ic": "maint", "date": "Sep 2026", "slug": "fall-furnace-checklist",
  "t": "Your fall furnace checklist", "x": "Five things to do before the first hard freeze.",
  "body": ["1. Change the furnace filter — a clogged filter is one of the most common causes of poor heat.",
           "2. Test your carbon monoxide and smoke alarms, including in the garage if you have a gas heater.",
           "3. Check that the outdoor intake and exhaust vents are clear and plan where snow will be piled.",
           "4. Turn the heat on before you need it to make sure everything works.",
           "5. Book a professional tune-up so combustion, safety controls and the heat exchanger are checked."]},
]

